1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,291
JAMIE: Tesla's recalling thousands of Model Ys because owners are missing a sticker—a literal sticker.

2
00:00:05,291 --> 00:00:34,392
ALEX: S&P up zero point four-seven percent to seven-thousand-four-hundred-eighty, Nasdaq at twenty-six-thousand-four-hundred-thirteen, also basically flat—zero point four-six percent. The real story is what's moving underneath: Merck just exploded four point seven-four percent on what I assume is either a drug approval or a CEO apology tour, and Costco got absolutely demolished, down two point three-six percent, which is wild because Costco is supposed to be the one company that survives a nuclear winter. Apparently not this week.

3
00:00:34,392 --> 00:00:40,061
JAMIE: Wait, why is Costco getting hit that hard? That's not normal volatility—that's a statement.

4
00:00:40,061 --> 00:01:03,871
ALEX: Earnings miss on margins, which is the Costco equivalent of finding out your gym buddy skipped leg day. The V I X is creeping up zero point five-four percent to sixteen point eighty-five, which is the market's way of saying, "I'm fine, everything's fine," while sweating slightly. The dollar's up, oil's up, Bitcoin's down, so crypto chose violence today for no reason.

5
00:01:03,871 --> 00:01:25,413
MAYA: Crypto chose violence—that's its baseline personality. But Merck up four point seven-four, is that a real catalyst or is this the market just picking random winners out of a hat? Because I watched Tesla pop two point one-nine percent on the back of a sticker recall and I've lost all confidence in what moves stocks.

6
00:01:25,413 --> 00:01:49,223
ALEX: Merck's probably a real drug approval or a buyback signal. Tesla's up because the market has the attention span of a golden retriever with a TikTok addiction—it saw "Tesla" and "news" in the same sentence and bought first, read never. Nvidia's down one point one-one percent, Apple's up one point four-seven, which is the market saying, "We love you, but we're hedging."

7
00:01:49,223 --> 00:02:06,230
JAMIE: So we've got Costco in freefall, Merck printing money, and Tesla somehow surviving its own sticker-based existential crisis. This is the market saying earnings don't matter, narrative does. This is the Crocs IPO of stock behavior—completely divorced from fundamentals, running on pure vibes.

8
00:02:06,230 --> 00:02:17,946
MAYA: The Crocs IPO of stock behavior—I'm using that forever. So what's the macro read? Are we actually okay or is this the calm before the Fed does something unhinged?

9
00:02:17,946 --> 00:03:03,298
ALEX: Indices are flat, volatility is whispering instead of screaming, and the Treasury curve is basically shrugging. We're in the "Fed might hike, Fed might not hike, Fed might just show up to the next meeting and read poetry" phase. Until inflation gives us a real signal, we're just watching the V I X twitch and calling it market analysis. So Christopher Waller, the Fed governor who apparently never learned the word "no," just said on the record that he can't rule out another rate hike further down the road if inflation persists. Which is the Fed's way of saying, "We reserve the right to destroy your mortgage refinance plans at any moment, but we're being coy about it."

10
00:03:03,298 --> 00:03:16,148
JAMIE: Wait—didn't we just spend the last six months pricing in rate cuts? The market's been operating on the assumption that the Fed's done hiking, and now Waller's out here saying, "Actually, maybe not"?

11
00:03:16,148 --> 00:03:30,888
ALEX: The market priced in cuts by end of year. We're now in mid-May, inflation's still running hot, and Waller just yanked the emergency brake on that narrative. It's the Fed's signature move: say nothing with maximum authority.

12
00:03:30,888 --> 00:03:44,871
MAYA: So Waller's basically saying, "Inflation persists, we hike again"—which is not a prediction, that's just the definition of monetary policy. Why is this news? Why is he out here doing a press tour about concepts?

13
00:03:44,871 --> 00:03:58,099
JAMIE: Because the market's been in denial! Consumers are already panicking, savings rates are collapsing, and the Fed's been quietly saying, "Yeah, we might have to do this again," and nobody wanted to hear it.

14
00:03:58,099 --> 00:04:18,886
ALEX: Waller's comment is the Fed equivalent of your doctor saying, "I can't rule out that you need surgery further down the road if your symptoms persist." It's not a diagnosis, it's a threat wrapped in bureaucratic language. The market heard "rate hike" and immediately priced out the cut that nobody was actually expecting anyway.

15
00:04:18,886 --> 00:04:35,893
MAYA: So this is just theater—Waller's out here keeping the door open because the Fed's terrified of looking like it caved to market pressure, and the market's terrified of actually believing him because that would require selling, which is harder than just scrolling and hoping.

16
00:04:35,893 --> 00:04:52,522
JAMIE: This is the Crocs IPO of monetary policy—completely untethered from what's actually happening. Inflation's still above target, the Fed's hiking again if it needs to, and everyone's pretending this is a surprise. Waller just confirmed what the data's been screaming for months.

17
00:04:52,522 --> 00:05:16,710
ALEX: The real tell is that the Treasury market moved on this. If the Fed actually had credibility on the "we're done hiking" narrative, Waller's comment would've been noise. Instead, traders just realized the Fed's not bluffing—they'll hike again if they have to, and inflation's still the boss. We're not in a "Fed's out of ammo" world. We're in a "Fed's just reloading" world.

18
00:05:16,710 --> 00:05:22,379
MAYA: The literary world is losing the plot because AI writes faster than lawyers can litigate.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/936073/ai-writing-granta-commonwealth-prize

19
00:05:22,379 --> 00:05:37,874
ALEX: So the defense mechanism is legal action, which takes years, while the thing you're suing has already written thousands more stories and is now teaching other AIs to write. This is like suing a tsunami for getting your house wet.

20
00:05:37,874 --> 00:05:51,102
MAYA: Exactly—publishers are in court, but by the time a judge rules, AI will have trained on more material. The literary establishment is operating on a slower timeline while the technology is already ahead.

21
00:05:51,102 --> 00:06:00,550
JAMIE: But the actual issue is real, right? These companies scraped billions of words without permission. That's not a tech problem, that's a copyright problem.

22
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:13,778
ALEX: It's a real copyright problem that will be solved years from now and by then the model will be obsolete. The publishers should've sued earlier when this started, not now when it's already won.

23
00:06:13,778 --> 00:06:20,203
MAYA: The literary world didn't prepare for this because they thought AI would be bad at metaphor.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/936073/ai-writing-granta-commonwealth-prize

24
00:06:20,203 --> 00:06:34,942
ALEX: Publishers are suing for training data, but the real damage is that AI doesn't need permission to be better at their job than their job is. That's the lawsuit nobody's filing because it's not illegal to be competent.

25
00:06:34,942 --> 00:06:45,147
JAMIE: Okay, switching gears—B J's Wholesale just reported earnings, and the story is brutally simple: cheap gas is the only thing keeping consumer spending alive right now.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/bjs-wholesales-earnings-show-that-cheap-gas-matters-for-people-8469d937?mod=mw_rss_topstories

26
00:06:45,147 --> 00:07:03,288
ALEX: B J's same-store sales were up, but that's entirely because gas prices dropped and people filled up their tanks. Strip out fuel and the company's basically flat. Savings rates are collapsing, and the only reason consumers aren't in freefall is because they're paying less at the pump.

27
00:07:03,288 --> 00:07:17,271
MAYA: So this is the real recession signal—not a crash, just a slow-motion surrender where people can only afford to buy stuff because gas got cheaper, not because their wages went up or their balance sheets improved.

28
00:07:17,271 --> 00:07:30,121
ALEX: Remove the gas discount and B J's numbers go negative. That's your consumer health check. The moment oil stabilizes or goes up again, you see the actual demand picture, and it's not pretty.

29
00:07:30,121 --> 00:07:40,325
JAMIE: So the Fed's sitting here talking about maybe hiking again, and they're not seeing this—that consumers are already maxed out and only surviving on fuel arbitrage?

30
00:07:40,325 --> 00:07:58,466
ALEX: The Fed's looking at inflation data and labor reports. They're not looking at B J's Wholesale earnings, which is where the actual consumer is screaming. Cheap gas is a tax cut for people living paycheck to paycheck, and the moment it goes away, so does discretionary spending.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/bjs-wholesales-earnings-show-that-cheap-gas-matters-for-people-8469d937?mod=mw_rss_topstories

31
00:07:58,466 --> 00:08:26,811
MAYA: This is the most depressing version of "at least the gas is cheap"—it's not a positive, it's the only thing preventing a negative. That's not consumer strength, that's consumer life support. Nigel Farage got hit with a nine thousand four hundred pound County Court judgment for unpaid debt, and Reform UK says he's appealing because he wasn't aware of the claim—which is a wild way to say "I ignore my mail."

32
00:08:26,811 --> 00:08:43,819
ALEX: A sitting politician just got legally declared a debtor because he didn't show up to court. The judgment was issued in his absence, which means either his lawyers are asleep or he genuinely thought ignoring a County Court summons would make it go away.

33
00:08:43,819 --> 00:08:49,110
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
